{"ts": 1789778935.3258822, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted: long feature 2 + long feature 29, predicted OOS Sharpe 1.05 (sd 0.33)\n\n**Search.** Swept all 40 features individually, then all pairs and triples built on the leaders.\n\n- Individually, only **#2 (0.912)** and **#29 (0.900)** stood out; the rest fell within |0.5|, mostly noise-level.\n- The pair **{2, 29} = 1.273** \u2014 well above the ~1.07 you'd get from naively averaging two correlated 0.9 signals. Genuine complementarity.\n- Best triple **{2, 29, 36} = 1.332**.\n\n**Why I left the higher in-sample number on the table.** I picked #36 as the winner of ~38 candidate third features that all landed in a narrow 1.04\u20131.33 band. With n=5000, the standard error on a Sharpe is ~0.22, so a maximum over that many noisy draws is inflated by roughly 0.15\u20130.25 \u2014 larger than the 0.06 the triple actually gained. Feature 36's edge is consistent with being pure selection artifact, so adding it likely *costs* out-of-sample return while diluting the two signals that are real.\n\nThe pair's advantage is a different kind of evidence: it's superadditive rather than max-selected, and it held up across every combination tested.\n\n**Two caveats on the prediction.** My 1.05 mean shrinks the 1.273 for the mild selection still present in the pair (top-2 of 40). The sd of 0.33 is deliberately wider than pure sampling error, because the dominant risk isn't estimation noise \u2014 it's that whatever economic relationship links #2 and #29 decays or inverts out of sample. That's a regime risk the in-sample data cannot price, and it's why I'd treat the downside tail as fatter than a symmetric interval implies."}
